kind of suggests that  the individual CTV's along the axons are  probably going to the synapses and  helping in a presynaptic  neurotransmitter release so I would like  to end with with with this  acknowledgment slide with all my  previous lab mates and my mentor dr. Roy  in particular and I would like to also  thank my collaborators who at the  Scripps Research Institute and NC Mir  UCSD and I thank you all for your  attention and I can take questions  whatever questions you have thank you so  much thank you dr. Ganguly for that  informative presentation we will now  start the live Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question drop-down box  located on the far left of your  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on the screen  and click the send button once there's  many of your questions we type or  so let's get started our first question  is what is the difference between slow  and fast transport and what makes slow  transport slow that's actually a great  question and - not much is known about  slow transport but I think one  interesting difference which we have  uncovered about slow axonal transport is  that slow axonal transport is modified  you can say like a modified form of fast  axonal transport so in fast axonal  transport the motors always are  associated with this vesicle and this  interaction is very long range or  persistent over time whereas in slow  transport what our studies have  uncovered is that the motors and the  vesicles you know you can call these  cargo complexes or cargo vesicles the  contraction time is very transient so  what actually happens is that these  vesicles or these cargos keep attaching  and detaching  from the the motors which move them  along the axon so in effect what happens  is that it's essentially moving by fast  transport but just because these  interactions are so transient and that  that causes like the cargo to move X  amount of microns over a much longer  time and that's what we think is making  it slower it's not and and we actually  don't know much about what mediates the  interaction or what kind of controls  these transient interactions between the  slow cargos and the motors but we do  know that the slow cargos move via these  motors which are also employed in fast  transport so I think it's just the  interaction and the nature of the  interactions which makes fast and slow  transport so did dr. Ganguly our next  question is is the axonal clathrin same  as clathrin-coated Piltz that's again a  very good question and to be honest i  don't really know the exact answer for  it i mean from our electron microscopy  studies as i mentioned  in my presentation it looks like these  are coated pits now we don't know for  sure whether these are endosit vesicles  or coated pits as you know the ones  which are responsible for transferring  acceptor uptake at the cell membrane we  have the composition of this clathrin  complex and we see proteins which are  not present in the core the endocytic  pits so we believe that these are  clathrin-coated complexes but we don't  know for sure whether these are coated  pits or not so you know right now to the  best of our understanding we think that  this is a unique complex which is just  transported along the axons and it's  independent of the coated pits what is  the role of microtubules and actin  filaments in the transport of Katherine  yeah that's a that's a good question and  we actually if you remember one of my  slides we show that I actually show the  data to show that if you disrupt the  microtubules you essentially stop the  slow transport of clattering along the  axon which is kind of consistent with  what we know because the the molecular  motors need these microtubule filaments  to you know move along or walk along or  whatever you call the motion to be so if  you disrupt these by using some kind of  toxins if you disrupt the microtubules  you are essentially going to stop the  movement of the motors and if you stop  the movement of the motors you're going  to stop the movement of clattering so in  effect if we disrupt the microtubules we  don't see any movement of clathrin in  the antegrade direction but actin is  actually quite interesting so if you can  if you go back to the slide where I was  talking about Mars and six so now myosin  six is known to interact with actin  which all myosin six or all myosin  motors are known to interact with some  actin filaments so our hypothesis was  that if we disrupt the the myosin six  and the interaction with the actin  filaments we will see kind of a  increased transport or that's kind of  what we saw in the presentation so we  saw that if you disrupt my  you see the there's a increased rate of  transport for clattering but when we did  the same experiments and we disrupted  actin we did not see that much of a  dramatic effect so I don't really know  whether disrupting actin will you know  stop transport or enhance transport but  if we inhibit myosin which is known to  interact with actin we do see a increase  in our collateral transport so we think  it may be not just the actin but there  may be like molecular motors or players  which are you know essential for the  interaction of this complex with that in  filaments and like Marcin six and if we  disrupt these complexes or these  proteins the effect would be more  dramatic than rather rather than just  disrupting the acting actin filaments  and dr. Ganguly it looks like we have  time for one more question have you  tested the role of other proteins in the  clathrin complex  besides myosin 6 on clathrin transport  yeah again that's that's a that's a  great question and we are actually  working in the lab so or two you know  like pursue that question and you know  we have this mass spec data which I  talked about in the presentation and we  have a huge list we kind of started with  myosin six because it seemed like it  wouldn't it's known to interact with  actin and then you know like we kind of  hypothesize that if we inhibit this  interaction we may see an increase in  transport or something would happen but  there are so many molecules which are  there in the complex it's not possible  to test every one of them but we have  tried our testing a few heat-shock  proteins which you know in a in a  previous study from our lab we show that  these are essential for the composition  of the slow transport complexes but we  didn't really see an effect of  disruption of heat shock proteins on the  transport of clathrin now it may be  because you know like the inhibition was  not complete or we were probably  targeting  you know like a like a different form of  HSC 70 but we tried myosin six and HSC  70 but we are actively pursuing other  molecules which are present in this  pattern complex so that's an ongoing  investigation I would like to once again  thank dr. Ganguly for his presentation I  would also like to thank Lab Rats for  making today's educational webcast  possible before we go I won't to  everyone note that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through June of 2018 you will receive an  email from labyrinth letting you know  when this webcast will be available for  replay please share that announcement  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's event that's all for now thank  you for joining us and we hope to see  you again soon goodbye
 

